>
>> My question is: how does one maximize/optimize the desirable permanent  
>> intrinsics one could potentially acquire?  I suppose this depends on  

I took this question to be about a collection, not about survival.  I've
played games where I tried to give all of the artifacts I could - All of
the coaligned and unaligned ones by massive sacrifests plus the ones
that can come into the game by other means.  I've played games where I
had a collection of dragon scales of each color, or at least one
valuable gem of each type, of all 9 Nazgul rings.  Some of the
resistances have very little effect on survival.

> It's quite normal for people to overprepare before their first
> ascension, but your wizmode list looks egregious. As others have said,
> reflection and magic resistance are the crucical ones. I'd say you
> should get poison resistance before you enter the Mines,

If it's about survival then poison resistance is the first major goal in
the early game.  The next depends on if you do the mines or sokoban
next - Reflection ends up extremely important later in the game.  My
experience is I get poison resistance no matter which order I do them.

> and try to
> get a complete set of elemental resistances (fire, cold, sleep, shock,
> poison) and some means of levitation before you enter Gehennom. For
> the ascension run, you will optionally want free action and conflict
> (both extrinsic).

Fire, cold, sleep and shock I take as I get them.  Not playing a speed
game is enough for me to have them by the time I hit the Castle.

> More important are other things in the game - good judgement, armor
> class, unicorn horn, escape items, etc. You don't always need a
> complete set. I'd like to refer you first to Maniac's Ascensions Guide
> and later to The Wiki.

Not just armor class but types of armor.  I value an MC#3 cloak and
there are folks who like displacement just as much.  One of the
advantages of being in no hurry is there's an extremely high chance of
at least an elven cloak because of the number of elves in the game. 
Just like unhurried players tend to build up a quiver of elven daggers
you'll end up with either an elven cloak or elven boots if your class
does not get stealth as you advance.

> It helps to keep book how you die. Take a long, hard look at that
> list, learn your lessons, and make sure you stick to them.

Judgment beats all types of equipment.  Consider all of the posts that
save scumming does not work.  Consider all of the posts that report that
an early wish for dragon scale mail does not work.  If you have bad
strategy no amount of help from items will keep you from getting into
fatal trouble eventually.

>> Of course, the followup question is: having gained as many desirable  
>> permanent properties as possible intrinsically, how do you fill the gaps  
>> with extrinsics, given that there will inevitably be tradeoffs (e.g. boots  
>> of speed vs jumping)?
>
> This is highly situational. But the specific question speed boots vs.
> jumping boots has a clear answer: speed boots.

Jumping boots have there uses on specific levels.  I tend to keep them
in the bag if I have both speed and jump boots.  As opposed to gloves
where I'll take dexterity over power even with a valkyrie if I have both.

I wand an amulet of reflection - It lets me target gray dragon scale
mail for MR and if I use the "oLS I'm already in enough trouble that
I'll likely die twice anyways.  So I want GDSM not SDSM if I have a
choice in the matter.  Having MR from dragon scales I don't need it from
a cloak so I most often want a robe for the MC#3 and the spell casting
benefit.  I want speed boots over stealth/elven boots over jump over
water walking, but I'll keep the water walking or levitation boots in
the bag for specific uses.  I'll keep a mummy wrapping in the bag until
I'm certain there will be no stores.  I'll want dexterity over power
gloves nearly every game.  All of these are personal preferences where
there are folks who have very good reasons for disagreeing with me.

For getting killed there's more benefit to learning how to use corridors
adn retreating a level than increasing armor to be in a bigger fatal
crowd.  There's more benfit to learning missile combat than how to wade
into a fatal crowd to do melee.

